  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost and freezing conditions west of the Blue \nRidge tonight through Tuesday night.\n\nAs of 10 AM, earlier fog is in the process of dissipating. Any \nlingering cloud cover should quickly erode as a cold front \nsweeps through the area early this morning, with mostly sunny \nconditions expected through the afternoon. NW winds pick up \nbehind the front with afternoon gusts to around 20 to 25 mph. A \nstrong area of Canadian high pressure pushes in from the Midwest\nthis afternoon before settling over the local area tonight. \nThis sets the stage for the first widespread frost event, \nmainly west of the Blue Ridge. High elevation valleys could even\nnear the freezing mark. For east of the Blue Ridge, forecast \nlows will be in the 40s.\n\nHigh pressure maintains its influence over the area on Tuesday \nwhich brings cool temps, lighter winds, and sunny skies. \nTue night also looks chilly, but fcst temps have come up a tad \ngiven light sly flow around the departing high. At this point \ntemps would be just mild enough to avoid another frost event. \nFor most, the fcst lows are in the low/mid 40s.\n\nRemaining dry Wed with high pressure continuing to move east\nyielding a S'ly return flow. Temps Wed into the 70s for most,\ncooler in the mtns.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry finish to the work week before rain chances\nreturn late in the weekend.\n\nThe northern branch of the jet stream remains amplified throughout \nthe mid/late week period. One embedded sw will drive another \ndry cold front through the region on Thu before another round \nof high pressure returns by Fri and into portions of next \nweekend. This maintains the dry weather pattern through at least\nSat. Initial high temps start off in the mid/upper 70s on \nThu before falling back into the 60s to low 70s for the \nweekend. Overnight conditions remain seasonable without any \nthreat of frost or freeze at this time.\n\nThe pattern late in the weekend becomes more interesting as a \ndisturbance across the Gulf of America lifts nwrd toward the \nGulf Coast region. Depending on what remains of this feature and\nthe quality of the moisture, an uptick in rainfall chances can \nbe expected for Sun. The latest forecast package calls for a 30\nto 50 percent chance of rain, but high uncertainty.",
